# The ecosystem source, brought in as read-only reference.
#
# Each of these is its own repository with its own history and remote; they are
# components this workspace reads and drives, never files it owns. Committing
# them here would fork them by accident.
#
# ANCHORED. Unanchored, `onlykey/` matches a directory of that name at ANY
# depth -- which silently swallowed `ecosystem/onlykey/`, the tracked data that
# describes this ecosystem's tasks and commands. Nothing errors when that
# happens: the files exist, the tool loads them, and they are simply never
# committed, so the fresh clone the seam exists to serve gets nothing.
/onlykey/
#
# `/workspace/*` rather than `/workspace/`, because of the exception below. An
# ignored DIRECTORY is never descended into, so a later `!` for something inside
# it does nothing at all -- and does it silently. Ignoring the children instead
# leaves the directory itself readable, so the negation can be seen.
/workspace/*

# The exception: this project's provisioning scripts, which the dashboard serves
# on top of its own. They are the only part of the system that is ours to keep --
# the rest of workspace/ is checkouts with their own remotes. Unversioned they
# were also undocumented, since what they carry is the REASONING: why the memory
# setting is needed at all, and why the USB gadget is deliberately absent so a
# provisioning script stops reinventing it badly.
!/workspace/provision/

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local machine configuration -- names paths, VM names and a shared token that
# are specific to one workstation. config.example.json is the tracked template.
dashboard/config.json

# Dashboard runtime state (run logs, transcripts, live output)
dashboard/state/

# Gate runtime state (declared sets, merge manifests). Same reasoning as above:
# process state, not an artifact. The durable record of a merge is its merge
# commit message, which lives in each repo's history alongside the change --
# the manifest exists so recovery has exact SHAs rather than a reconstruction.
gate/state/

# A fork's own component addresses, and any repo that exists only on this
# machine. Each ecosystem's repos.json is tracked and holds the upstream ones,
# so pointing the gate at your forks by editing it would mean conflicting with
# every upstream pull, forever, over a purely local change. URLs only --
# structure stays tracked. ecosystem/repos.local.example.json is the template.
#
# Per ecosystem, beside the registry it overrides: one ecosystem's local
# addresses must not apply to another's repos.
ecosystem/*/repos.local.json

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
desktop.ini
push/
scratch/
legacy/

